WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.229
Think of a recipe card called make tea. Whenever you want tea, you follow the same card. You never rewrite it. In a program, a subprogram is a named block of code that does one job, and you can call it whenever you need it.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:27.131
A function is a subprogram that sends a value back, using return. Area takes a length and a width, and returns length times width. Rug equals area of three and two, so rug is six.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:34.228
A procedure carries out a task but does not have to send a value back. Show menu just prints two lines.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:49.371
P length and p width are parameters. A parameter is a named input in the subprogram's header. Parameters let one subprogram work with different data each time, so it can be reused: area of five and four gives twenty.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:01:06.825
Some subprograms come ready-made. Built-in ones, like print and len, are part of Python. A library is a collection of pre-written subprograms that you import, like random. Library code has already been tested, so it is less likely to contain errors.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:19.321
First decide which idea is being tested. For a difference between a function and a procedure, use the return value: a function returns a value, a procedure does not have to.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:29.671
For a similarity, choose shared features, such as parameters, local variables and reuse. For an explain question, give a point, then so, then its effect.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:40.549
Here is a real question from Paper 2 in November 2020. Explain one difference between a function and a procedure. It is worth two marks.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:55.137
Think about what happens when each one finishes. A function ends with return and hands a value back, so the value can be stored, like rug equals area of three and two. A procedure just does its job, like showing a menu.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:08.927
So write: a function always sends a value back to the code that called it, whereas a procedure does not have to. One mark for each half. This explanation is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:19.565
A common mistake is saying a procedure cannot take parameters. It can. Both can take parameters. Only the return value tells them apart.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:30.475
Another mistake is writing libraries are quicker, with no reason. Say why: the code is already written and tested, so it saves time and has fewer errors.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:42.459
Now you try. Look at these two subprograms. Which one is a function, and which is a procedure? Name one parameter, and give a reason for your answer. Pause and decide.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:55.931
Double has a return line, so it sends a value back: it is a function. Double of seven returns fourteen. Greet only prints, so it is a procedure. The parameters are p num and p name.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:07.316
Remember the method: a function returns a value, a procedure does a task, and parameters are the named inputs in the header. Now try the practice questions for this type.
